                <head>No. 799<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi></head>
                <p>On March 3, President Truman approved a Department of State proposal to invite
                    Austrian Chancellor Leopold Figl to Washington for a visit in May; these plans
                    were made public in a press release issued on March 31. On April 30, the
                    Department of State issued a press release announcing Figl’s itinerary which
                    included arrival in New York on May 11, a visit to Washington from May 12
                    through May 16, a tour to Williamsburg, Niagara Falls, Detroit, Chicago, and
                    Madison, ending in New York with departure scheduled for May 27. For text of
                    these press releases, see Department of State <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bulletin</hi>, April 14, 1952, page 585, and <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >ibid</hi>., May 12, 1952, page 746.</p>
                <pb facs="0549" n="1749" xml:id="pg1749"/>
                <p>Copies of the briefing papers, itinerary, and biographical sketches prepared for
                    Figl’s visit are in Conference files, lot 59 D 95, CF 107. For a record of
                    Figl’s meeting with Secretary of State Acheson and with President Truman on May
                    13, see telegram 2859 to Vienna, May 14, <ref target="#d800"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">infra</hi></ref>. For a record of a meeting between
                    Austrian officials and representatives of the Department of State on May 14, see
                    the memorandum of conversation by Rutter, <ref target="#d801">Document
                    801</ref>. For a summary of Figl’s conversation with the Director for Mutual
                    Security, W. Averell Harriman, on May 15, see Mustos 370 and 371, Documents <ref
                        target="#d802">802</ref> and <ref target="#d803">803</ref>.</p>
            </div>
